  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "CLI's fee franchise keeps compounding at high single digits as listed REIT acquisitions, private funds (SCCP, Wingate) and Ascott's signed pipeline convert to FUM and fees, but the S$200bn FUM target slips a couple of years. The REIB book is recycled at roughly 10-15% below carrying value (China weighted), proceeds pay down debt rather than creating new value. Fee business EV of ~S$7bn plus the haircut investment book less S$6.6bn net debt and NCI.",
    "bull": "The asset-light pivot lands: S$200bn FUM by ~2029, >S$1bn operating earnings around 2030, Ascott to S$500m fees, and China C-REIT listings let CLI exit China balance-sheet assets near book. FRB margins scale toward listed-funds levels (~44%). Investment book still valued at the same haircut book (bridge is common to all scenarios), so bull upside is understated if sponsor REIT stakes re-rate above NAV.",
    "bear": "APAC real-estate capital raising stalls again and China keeps bleeding: FRB growth slows to ~3%, event fees from the H1 2026 divestment wave do not repeat, and margins stay around 34-35% under growth spend. The bridge's 10-15% haircut on the investment book proves too small -- each further 10% haircut on the S$18.3bn REIB carrying value costs ~S$0.36/share, which this scenario does not include."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Sum of parts. The fee business is the asset the market should eventually pay a manager multiple for; it is valued on owner FCF with SBC expensed. The investment book is not a cash-flow stream to project -- it is a portfolio of mostly listed sponsor stakes and China-heavy property marked at a discount to book, consistent with recent divestment pricing. Consolidated net debt (incl. leases) and NCI are deducted once. Reported PATMI is dominated by revaluation and portfolio-gain noise and is not used.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "REIB haircut: the S$18.3bn associates/JVs/IP carrying value dominates the bridge; each 10% haircut change is ~S$0.36/share",
      "China: ~two-thirds of the S$7-9bn non-core pool is in China, where divestments have cleared 13-20% below book",
      "Event-driven fees: H1 2026 FRB revenue (+18%) included an India performance fee and divestment/acquisition fees that may not recur",
      "Gearing: net debt/equity rose 0.35x -> 0.43x -> 0.45x while recycling lagged",
      "Ticker collision: Yahoo C09.SI is City Developments; any automated price refresh on C09.SI writes CDL's price into this file"
    ]
  },
